      "topic": {
        "text": "Spam classification using machine learning",
        "refs": ["Q0000", "Q0001", "Q0002", "Q0003", "Q0004", "Q0005", "Q0006", "Q0007", "Q0008", "Q0009", "Q0010", "Q0011"],
        "claims": [
          {
            "text": "Google classifies emails as spam or non‑spam by learning patterns from large collections of labeled emails.",
            "basis": "explicit",
            "availability": "SOURCE_SUPPORTED",
            "literal_kind": "verbal",
            "instructional_role": null,
            "refs": ["Q0000", "Q0001"],
            "related_refs": []
          },
          {
            "text": "A human can be trained to recognize spam by reviewing thousands of examples and memorising patterns such as urgent language or suspicious sender IDs.",
            "basis": "explicit",
            "availability": "SOURCE_SUPPORTED",
            "literal_kind": "verbal",
            "instructional_role": null,
            "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003", "Q0004"],
            "related_refs": []
          },
          {
            "text": "Machine learning follows the same idea: a computer program is trained on many spam and non‑spam emails to learn those patterns.",
            "basis": "explicit",
            "availability": "SOURCE_SUPPORTED",
            "literal_kind": "verbal",
            "instructional_role": null,
            "refs": ["Q0005", "Q0006"],
            "related_refs": []
          },
          {
            "text": "Training is the phase where the model learns from labeled data; inference is the phase where the trained model predicts the label for new, unseen emails.",
            "basis": "explicit",
            "availability": "SOURCE_SUPPORTED",
            "literal_kind": "verbal",
            "instructional_role": null,
            "refs": ["Q0010", "Q0011"],
            "related_refs": []
          },
          {
            "text": "This process is analogous to a traditional program that computes a fixed formula (e.g., y = x²) for given inputs, whereas a machine‑learning model derives its own mapping from data.",
            "basis": "explicit",
            "availability": "SOURCE_SUPPORTED",
            "literal_kind": "verbal",
            "instructional_role": null,
            "refs": ["Q0011"],
            "related_refs": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "The teaching explains that spam detection can be understood as pattern learning: a human can be trained on many examples to recognize spam, and similarly a machine‑learning model is trained on labeled emails to learn patterns, after which it can infer the label of new messages.",
        "refs": ["Q0000", "Q0001", "Q0002", "Q0003", "Q0004", "Q0005", "Q0006", "Q0010", "Q0011"],
        "claims": [
          {
            "text": "Training a model on labeled spam and non‑spam emails enables it to detect patterns and classify new emails.",
            "basis": "direct_entailment",
            "availability": "SOURCE_SUPPORTED",
            "literal_kind": "verbal",
            "instructional_role": "prerequisite",
            "refs": ["Q0005", "Q0006", "Q0010"],
            "related_refs": []
          },
          {
            "text": "Inference uses the learned patterns to predict whether a new email is spam.",
            "basis": "direct_entailment",
            "availability": "SOURCE_SUPPORTED",
            "literal_kind": "verbal",
            "instructional_role": "prerequisite",
            "refs": ["Q0010", "Q0011"],
            "related_refs": []
          }
        ]
      },
